  "live_records": {
    "q-84265": {
      "dom_id": "step-q84265",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)={e}^{t-1}.$ At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=2.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=2?$",
      "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=2$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,2].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(2)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{2}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =2+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{[{e}^{t-1}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2+({e}^{2-1}-{e}^{1-1}) \\\\  & =2+({e}^{1}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2+e-1 \\\\  & =e+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=2$ is $x=e+1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
