    "q-84317": {
      "dom_id": "step-q84317",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14181002/assets/09d2770a261ed886c69b1e555adc759a7d427aa8898565c6a7ec60477ae39d41.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=4.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=6?$",
      "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=4$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=6.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(6)=4+4=8.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=8.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
