    "q-121018": {
      "dom_id": "step-q121018",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14181002/assets/28e5f037ac5479595be52397232365b1b3c03012dcb5f0f6d187e149ba8ef33f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=3.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=6?$",
      "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(1)=3,$ and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=1$ to $t=6.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(6)=3+1=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
