    "q-351445": {
      "dom_id": "step-q351445",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)={sec}^{2}⁡(t).$ At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=-2.$ \nFind the position of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-2+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2+{[\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-2+1-0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is $x=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
